  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nPrecip Chances / Trends: The dry stretch of weather will continue\ninto Friday as a potent shortwave trough and low pressure system \ntrack into northern MN. We should get some mid and high clouds \nfrom this system, but the atmosphere looks too dry to get any rain\nfrom it. Then, as another shortwave drops into the northern \nPlains and a frontal boundary approaches, chances for rain will \nincrease from west to east on Saturday, with rain likely (75-90%) \nSaturday evening/night. Dry air could keep the east dry through \nthe evening hours, but eventually the dry air will give way to the\nslug of moisture (PWATs just over an inch) advancing east. Will \nneed to monitor if tropical activity off the Eastern Seaboard \ncould slow the pattern a little, delaying the system a little \nmore. Probability for at least 0.50\" of rain has increased to \n40-70%, highest across central and north-central WI, where this \nrain would help in the worsening drought conditions.\n\nCold air wrapping into the northern part of the system will\neventually change the rain over to snow Saturday night into Sunday,\nwith some lake effect/enhanced snow shower activity lingering into\nSunday evening as the system departs. Probabilities for 0.1\" of \nsnow have increased to 30-75% in north-central WI (highest in \nVilas Co.) with probabilities of an inch or more increasing to \n30-50% in the favored lake effect snow region of northwest Vilas \nCo., but quickly fall to zero by the time you get to Oneida Co. So\nat least a little snow is likely on the back side of this system,\nbut overall impacts look to be minor.\n\nWinds: Gusts to around 25 mph will continue through sunset today,\nthen reduced mixing will allow surface winds to decrease around/after\nsunset, with a few gusts to 20 mph possible through the night. As\nmixing increases Thursday morning and strong winds remain aloft,\nmodel soundings showing a breezy mid-late morning and early\nafternoon, with southwest winds gusting to 30-35 mph, with a few\ngusts to 40 kts possible. Winds aloft start to decrease Thursday\nafternoon as the system exits to our north/east, so winds will\ndiminish by mid-late afternoon, with light winds expected Thursday\nnight. Southeast to south winds pick up on Friday, gusting to\n~25 mph. The persistent gusty winds will keep waves rocking and \nrolling on Lake Michigan, with waves approaching 10 feet Saturday \nmorning.\n\nTemperatures: Well above normal temps will prevail into Saturday,\nwith highs in the 50s to mid 60s (normal highs this time of year\nare in the upper 30s to lower 40s). With a slightly slower \narriving system, Saturday could turn out to be the warmest day, \nespecially over eastern WI. Record highs in the upper 50s and \nlow 60s Friday into Saturday look to be in jeopardy, especially \nGreen Bay, Manitowoc and Sturgeon Bay. Several record high \nminimums look to be set on Saturday as well, with Friday night \nlows only dropping into upper 40s to low 50s, provided it does not\ncool off too fast before midnight. Much cooler air arrives behind\nthe low pressure system next week with highs tumbling in the 30s \nto around 40 and lows in the teens and 20s.",
